#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "11f5bedf-cfc8-5ace-b080-5ec30b406a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7436" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/0ae3d46a511cfe74b489eec33e6f6fec0adcb5be7d955a1880f979963b0e01f6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "29cfe1ff-ca64-51f9-8da1-5d0f798711f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7437" :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-64x \\\\ 4p & =-64 \\\\ p & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccc7f71-d2e0-5f35-80a5-042a53eda2da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "07678ad9-a16f-5fd2-8e39-85376aa9112e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7442" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3afa95b7-02e2-5ada-bc15-1419124e03ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285792} {:answer/id #uuid "5e742002-4a38-5ead-a25e-c6f22714768d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285793} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f5d15d-e732-5574-932f-1825df384cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285794} {:answer/id #uuid "f8434fc9-b25f-587e-a7fe-02ef17092fd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285795} {:answer/id #uuid "711ac830-b9b2-50d0-b382-c8924c3f09bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=t,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3afa95b7-02e2-5ada-bc15-1419124e03ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285792} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8795698-e693-5154-b430-5eaeb64341d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "827bc944-9468-5f1f-aaa1-d206a7a58e66" :question/math-academy-id "q-106412" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/c42f5aac98d31b7a66a60eebae34b5e83cb62f3ba4801595a81dd9bb57706384.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,-8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97bfb9ef-3b21-59a5-b2de-dbce50578c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285799} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf26b1b-c1e2-5d0b-9c3a-0d41490094fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285800} {:answer/id #uuid "a370caac-e18e-5e57-8775-60e2285abcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285801} {:answer/id #uuid "474b91e9-76ab-55bc-bf3e-6da4e94fe85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285802} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4f9285-417b-56b7-9d67-fdcf50a6ede0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3t,\\,\\,y=6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a370caac-e18e-5e57-8775-60e2285abcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285801} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6db4438-92a4-525c-881f-a8f8e59c4c86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "200bd8e0-2a01-528c-8a4d-6d1ad4880558" :question/math-academy-id "q-106420" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=12x?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =12x \\\\ 4p & =12 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c41ffe1b-e741-58bf-9844-83fbc6bc7030" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bed0c2b0-24f9-5ccd-aba1-0e03715b8e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285832} {:answer/id #uuid "75c2fe08-1bcf-5695-a7cc-2aca55b78d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed0c2b0-24f9-5ccd-aba1-0e03715b8e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285832} :answer-field/id #uuid "9292f618-20fc-5f5b-b651-d46d9c09e736" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285831} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8960adf9-f3a6-585b-b81a-c530c948f3dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8960adf9-f3a6-585b-b81a-c530c948f3dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285835} :answer-field/id #uuid "146d0acb-1c7a-5699-899c-0a0fafd6cc94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "864c98fc-1abc-5908-a417-e5cb64cfdbb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-280233" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/d8e4aa88acf2a0d69d4c4208dc4951a33cc92e59e177395ec74be3ae0f9a8c7f.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the parabola shown above. Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-4$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{4}{5}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{4}{5})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{8}{5}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e88d0df-06b6-5434-8ee7-e41bd662943d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-7t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285849} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd72ae6-89a1-57ef-890b-9a8b14f83058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-7{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285850} {:answer/id #uuid "19eb8185-2670-53bd-a4a1-8cf6bfc406b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285851} {:answer/id #uuid "aee65ba4-52fa-5592-9962-0538b650591b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-7t,\\,\\,y=-14{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285852} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce7f774-d75a-59d6-b1aa-0d0940d8bbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19eb8185-2670-53bd-a4a1-8cf6bfc406b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285851} :answer-field/id #uuid "4728aa8d-ba4c-59c6-bb37-aca322427c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51659ed2-0d6d-5995-abed-0cbe41395da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-84675" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-7,-7)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-7,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-7$ and $y=-7$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-7=2pt \\\\ -7=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{7}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =p{(-\\frac{7}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -7 & =p\\cdot \\frac{49}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -7 & =\\frac{49}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-7 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{7}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{7}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{7}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"}]}
